{{Nihongo|'''Constrained Capitalism'''|制限資本主義|Seigen Shihonshugi}} refers to the family of socioeconomic systems that emerged across the [[Sanese Archipelago]] during the mid-20th century. Characterized by the combination of market-oriented capitalist economies with extensive state intervention, social welfare frameworks, and regulatory oversight, these models transformed the three Sanese nations—[[Okaiken]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Yanbaru]]—from developing or war-devastated states into some of [[Adonia]]'s most advanced economies by the turn of the 21st century.

While sharing common origins in post-[[Great Adonian War]] reconstruction and earlier modernization efforts, each nation developed distinctive variants reflecting unique historical trajectories, political cultures, and geographic circumstances. The term has become central to comparative political economy discussions regarding the [[Fosian Tigers]] and alternative models to Western neoliberalism.

== Overview ==
The Sanese Archipelago's three sovereign nations adopted parallel but distinct approaches to economic development during the mid-20th century. All three systems share fundamental characteristics: competitive private enterprise combined with active state guidance, emphasis on export-oriented industrialization, and social welfare mechanisms intended to maintain stability during rapid economic transformation.

However, significant differences emerged in the balance between state and market, the structure of labor relations, the extent of welfare provisions, and the mechanisms of corporate governance. These differences reflect the divergent political histories of the three nations—from Okaiken's continuity with the [[Honda Shogunate]] bureaucratic tradition, to Nakamizu's [[Heisei Restoration]] reformism, to Yanbaru's maritime trading heritage as a vassal state of the [[Kingdom of Zong]].

