== By country ==
=== Okaiken ===
{{Main|Constrained Capitalism (Okaiken)}}

Okaiken's variant of Constrained Capitalism is distinguished by its close coordination between government ministries, financial institutions, and industrial conglomerates. The system emerged from the administrative traditions of the [[Honda Shogunate]] and was formalized during the [[Saito Restoration]] of the late 19th century, then significantly reshaped during post-war reconstruction.

The model is characterized by the ''keiretsu'' system of interlocking corporate shareholdings, lifetime employment traditions in major corporations, and enterprise unionism where labor unions are organized by company rather than industry. Unlike Nakamizu's welfare-heavy approach, Okaiken relies heavily on corporate paternalism—benefits such as housing allowances, healthcare, and retirement pensions are typically provided through employers rather than universal government programs.

Administrative guidance (''gyosei shido'') by ministries—particularly the [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry]]—has played a central role in directing industrial development, often through informal mechanisms rather than explicit legislation. The practice of ''amakudari'', where retired bureaucrats take positions in private corporations, ensures close state-business coordination.

The system achieved remarkable success during the [[Go-go 8090]] period of the 1980s, transforming Okaiken into one of Adonia's largest economies. However, the collapse of the bubble economy in the early 1990s and subsequent [[Lost Decades]] exposed structural weaknesses, leading to reforms including the [[Digital Infrastructure Act]] of 2008 and greater emphasis on labor market flexibility.

=== Nakamizu ===
{{Main|Constrained Capitalism (Nakamizu)}}

Nakamizu's Constrained Capitalism represents the most welfare-oriented variant among the three Sanese nations. Emerging from the [[Heisei Restoration]] and post-war reconstruction under Prime Minister [[Yamakawa Misao]], the system combines liberalized markets with an extensive social safety net inspired by social democratic models while incorporating [[Zong]]-styled trade policies.

The model features universal healthcare and near-universal social insurance coverage, extensive public investment in transportation and education, and progressive taxation supporting relatively high public spending. Unlike Okaiken's enterprise-based system, Nakamizu maintains industry-wide labor unions that negotiate across entire sectors, contributing to higher union density and more standardized wage agreements.

Political support for the model centers on the [[National Social Liberal Party]], which has advocated regulated market economics alongside expansive social programs. The system is widely credited with enabling the "Economic Ascension" (''Keizai Shōtenki'') from the 1960s through the 1990s, transforming Nakamizu from an impoverished nation into one of Adonia's leading technological and industrial powers.

Critics from market-liberal perspectives argue that the system's high taxation and regulatory burdens discourage entrepreneurial activity, while left-wing critics contend that neoliberal elements have contributed to rising inequality and labor casualization in recent decades.

=== Yanbaru ===
{{Main|Constrained Capitalism (Yanbaru)}}

Yanbaru's variant of Constrained Capitalism reflects the nation's distinctive history as a maritime trading state and former vassal of the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. Unlike the industrial conglomerates dominant in Okaiken or the welfare institutions of Nakamizu, Yanbaru's model emphasizes trade facilitation, family-owned trading houses, and community-based mutual aid networks.

The system emerged from Yanbaru's historical role as a commercial intermediary between Zong and the central Sanese states, formalized through the [[Maritime Accords of Okami]] in the 1630s which allowed Yanbaru to serve as Okaiken's gateway to international trade during periods of isolation. This legacy produced an economy oriented toward services, shipping, and tropical exports rather than heavy manufacturing.

Labor relations are characterized by regional trade guilds rather than enterprise or industry unions, while social welfare relies heavily on community-based systems and extended family networks—a reflection of the indigenous [[Shoto]]-influenced culture that emphasizes collective responsibility. The state maintains relatively limited direct intervention in the economy compared to Okaiken or Nakamizu, focusing primarily on trade licensing and port infrastructure.

The model has produced lower government spending as a percentage of GDP than its neighbors, with corresponding lower tax burdens. Critics argue this results in weaker social protections for vulnerable populations, while supporters emphasize the flexibility and entrepreneurial dynamism of the maritime-oriented system.

== Historical development ==
=== Common origins ===
The foundations of Constrained Capitalism across the archipelago can be traced to the transformative modernization efforts of the 19th century. In Okaiken, the [[Saito Restoration]] (1871–1890) dismantled feudal structures while preserving bureaucratic traditions. In Nakamizu, the [[Heisei Restoration]] initiated similar reforms with greater emphasis on social welfare. Yanbaru, already integrated into Zong's trade networks, modernized its commercial institutions while maintaining indigenous governance structures.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952) devastated Okaiken and significantly impacted Nakamizu, creating conditions for the post-war economic systems to emerge. All three nations prioritized reconstruction, industrialization, and export-oriented growth during the 1950s and 1960s, albeit through different institutional mechanisms.

=== Divergent paths ===
Despite common starting points, the three nations diverged significantly in their approaches. Okaiken's bureaucratic traditions produced a model centered on administrative guidance and corporate conglomerates. Nakamizu's reformist political culture emphasized welfare expansion and industry-wide labor coordination. Yanbaru's commercial heritage produced a trade-oriented variant with community-based social mechanisms.

These differences were reinforced by political developments: the conservative dominance of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] in Okaiken, the centrist governance of the [[National Social Liberal Party]] in Nakamizu, and the constitutional monarchy with merchant influence in Yanbaru.

=== Contemporary challenges ===
By the early 21st century, all three variants faced challenges including demographic aging, globalization pressures, and technological disruption. Okaiken's Lost Decades prompted significant reforms including corporate governance changes and limited welfare expansion. Nakamizu faced debates over maintaining high social spending amid slowing growth. Yanbaru navigated transitions in global trade patterns following changes in continental Fosian markets.

The three models continue to evolve, with some convergence in certain areas—Okaiken expanding welfare provisions, Nakamizu pursuing limited market reforms, and Yanbaru developing more formalized social protections—while maintaining distinctive core characteristics.

== Criticism and debate ==
Constrained Capitalism has attracted criticism from multiple ideological perspectives across the archipelago and internationally.

=== Market-liberal criticism ===
Free-market advocates argue that all three variants create excessive regulatory burdens and distort market mechanisms. The close government-business relationships in Okaiken, high taxation in Nakamizu, and trade licensing in Yanbaru have been criticized as impediments to competition and innovation.

=== Left-wing criticism ===
Progressive critics argue that the systems perpetuate inequality, particularly Okaiken's corporate-based welfare model that leaves non-regular employees without adequate protections. The limited welfare provisions in Yanbaru and the labor casualization trends in Nakamizu have also drawn criticism from socialist and labor movements.

=== Comparative criticism ===
International observers frequently compare the Sanese models with each other and with Western alternatives. The contrast between Okaiken's corporate paternalism and Nakamizu's welfare state has become a reference point in debates about the appropriate balance between efficiency and equity. Yanbaru's maritime-oriented model offers an alternative for nations with commercial rather than industrial traditions.

== Legacy ==
Constrained Capitalism is widely regarded as fundamental to the economic success of the Sanese Archipelago. The three variants—together with similar models elsewhere in [[Fosia]]—are often cited as evidence that alternative paths to development exist beyond Western neoliberal orthodoxy.

The models have influenced economic policy discussions across Adonia, with various nations seeking to adapt elements of the Sanese approaches to their own circumstances. Comparative studies of the three variants continue to inform debates about industrial policy, welfare design, and the role of the state in market economies.

